1
00:00:00,292 --> 00:00:04,516
1,170,523 tokens.

2
00:00:04,850 --> 00:00:09,993
That is how many tokens it takes
to describe 2,594 tools to a model.

3
00:00:10,488 --> 00:00:13,867
And we are not even talking about
using those tools, just describing

4
00:00:13,867 --> 00:00:15,629
them through plain MCP schemas.

5
00:00:16,251 --> 00:00:23,057
Cloudflare measured this on their own API
That is 585% of a 200K context window.

6
00:00:23,638 --> 00:00:24,430
Think about that.

7
00:00:24,690 --> 00:00:29,070
The tool definition alone are almost
six times larger than the entire context

8
00:00:29,070 --> 00:00:32,481
window Now, look at this tiny teal bar.

9
00:00:32,938 --> 00:00:36,476
Same API, same endpoints, but
using an approach called code mode.

10
00:00:36,632 --> 00:00:40,066
The context needed drops to around
just one thousand tokens, and

11
00:00:40,066 --> 00:00:41,111
that is a massive difference.

12
00:00:41,589 --> 00:00:45,504
And today, we are going to explore how
that works, what happened when we tried

13
00:00:45,544 --> 00:00:49,936
a similar approach in our production
agent, and where we ran into challenges.

14
00:00:51,078 --> 00:00:53,791
But before we jump in, a quick
question for everyone watching.

15
00:00:54,307 --> 00:00:57,269
How many MCP servers is your
agent connected to right now?

16
00:00:57,771 --> 00:00:58,946
Drop a number in the chat.

17
00:00:59,003 --> 00:01:01,077
We would love to hear what
your setup looks like.

18
00:01:02,077 --> 00:01:05,172
Hi, I'm Sonam, and joining
me today is Nishant.

19
00:01:05,545 --> 00:01:08,657
We both work at Adobe, and over
the past few months, we have

20
00:01:08,657 --> 00:01:12,804
been tackling a challenge that
comes up as the agents scale.

21
00:01:13,270 --> 00:01:16,683
The more tools you connect,
the slower, more expensive, and

22
00:01:16,702 --> 00:01:17,976
less accurate they can become.

23
00:01:18,608 --> 00:01:22,560
The numbers you see in our
title, one fifty K to twenty-two

24
00:01:22,567 --> 00:01:26,613
K, come from a real production
agent, not a synthetic benchmark.

25
00:01:27,139 --> 00:01:31,804
Today, we'll walk you through how we
achieve those results, how we measured

26
00:01:31,804 --> 00:01:39,292
them, and just as importantly, what
didn't work along the way So rather

27
00:01:39,294 --> 00:01:44,235
than just showing you the final numbers,
we want to share what actually helped

28
00:01:44,235 --> 00:01:49,302
us to get there and what you can take
back and apply to your own agents.

29
00:01:49,913 --> 00:01:53,301
We have broken today's talk
into three practical takeaways.

30
00:01:53,940 --> 00:01:55,216
First, the pattern.

31
00:01:55,655 --> 00:02:00,434
What happens when we show models our
tools as code instead of schemas?

32
00:02:01,587 --> 00:02:03,431
Second, the measurement.

33
00:02:03,873 --> 00:02:09,718
A simple way to evaluate your own agent
this afternoon using just one log line.

34
00:02:11,313 --> 00:02:12,502
Third, the command.

35
00:02:13,098 --> 00:02:15,488
Something you can try yourself tonight.

36
00:02:16,500 --> 00:02:21,829
And finally, we'll also talk about what
didn't work, where things broke, what we

37
00:02:21,829 --> 00:02:24,129
learned, and what we'd do differently.

38
00:02:24,565 --> 00:02:28,992
But before we get into any of that, let's
look at the problem that started it all.

39
00:02:30,093 --> 00:02:32,122
So where did all the context go?

40
00:02:32,634 --> 00:02:34,202
This is our agent's context window.

41
00:02:34,781 --> 00:02:37,759
Each square represents two
thousand tokens, and the

42
00:02:37,759 --> 00:02:39,153
entire grid is two hundred K.

43
00:02:40,209 --> 00:02:44,866
Our agent connects to everyday developer
tools: Outlook, SharePoint, Jira,

44
00:02:44,927 --> 00:02:46,997
Confluence, Slack, and a few others.

45
00:02:47,291 --> 00:02:48,067
Nothing unusual.

46
00:02:48,906 --> 00:02:50,781
Now watch what happen as we add them.

47
00:02:51,361 --> 00:02:54,301
Watch how the context starts filling
up as we connect all the tools.

48
00:02:54,976 --> 00:02:59,248
Each one takes up a chunk of the
available space, one fifty K tokens, and

49
00:02:59,248 --> 00:03:00,964
the user hasn't even typed a word yet.

50
00:03:01,176 --> 00:03:05,483
All of this is just tool definitions,
names, descriptions, and parameters.

51
00:03:06,454 --> 00:03:08,870
That's seventy-five percent of
the context window gone before

52
00:03:08,870 --> 00:03:10,226
the user even says hello.

53
00:03:11,202 --> 00:03:13,731
So why is so much context being consumed?

54
00:03:14,298 --> 00:03:18,267
This is Google Drive's get_document,
a simple tool definition with a

55
00:03:18,267 --> 00:03:21,065
name, description, and parameters.

56
00:03:21,870 --> 00:03:24,067
And here's Salesforce update_record.

57
00:03:24,358 --> 00:03:25,326
Again, pretty small.

58
00:03:26,163 --> 00:03:30,658
But the problem isn't one or two tools,
it's hundreds, sometimes thousands.

59
00:03:31,517 --> 00:03:35,096
Anthropic reports that agents
can process hundreds of thousands

60
00:03:35,096 --> 00:03:38,801
of tokens just loading the tool
definition before reading a request.

61
00:03:39,837 --> 00:03:43,724
And this happens on every request, whether
the user needs one tool or a hundred.

62
00:03:44,329 --> 00:03:48,019
It's a fixed context tax that you
pay before any real work begins.

63
00:03:49,804 --> 00:03:51,755
Now let us look at the
second failure mode.

64
00:03:51,822 --> 00:03:54,661
This one is less obvious
but often more expensive.

65
00:03:55,841 --> 00:03:59,567
Imagine you ask your agent to take a
meeting transcript from Google Drive

66
00:03:59,595 --> 00:04:01,894
and attach it to a Salesforce lead.

67
00:04:03,124 --> 00:04:06,839
First, the agent calls get_document,
and the entire transcript gets

68
00:04:06,839 --> 00:04:08,486
loaded into the model's context.

69
00:04:09,506 --> 00:04:13,262
Then it calls update_record, and
the model has to write that same

70
00:04:13,262 --> 00:04:15,212
transcript again as an argument.

71
00:04:15,973 --> 00:04:18,541
So the same data passes
through the model twice.

72
00:04:20,152 --> 00:04:25,094
For a two-hour sales meeting, Anthropic
estimates around 50,000 extra tokens.

73
00:04:25,497 --> 00:04:29,318
And with large documents, there's
also a higher chance of errors when

74
00:04:29,325 --> 00:04:30,789
copying the data between the tools.

75
00:04:31,753 --> 00:04:35,736
Essentially, we are using an
expensive AI model just to copy and

76
00:04:35,739 --> 00:04:37,312
paste data from one tool to another.

77
00:04:39,265 --> 00:04:41,358
But token consumption
wasn't our only problem.

78
00:04:41,437 --> 00:04:43,484
We noticed something even more concerning.

79
00:04:44,364 --> 00:04:45,188
42%.

80
00:04:46,015 --> 00:04:49,005
That is how often our agent
selected the right tool.

81
00:04:49,873 --> 00:04:52,126
Not the right answer, just the right tool.

82
00:04:52,926 --> 00:04:54,876
More than half the time,
it picked the wrong one.

83
00:04:56,049 --> 00:04:57,954
And Anthropic highlights
a similar problem.

84
00:04:58,611 --> 00:05:02,524
Models often struggle with tool
selection and incorrect parameters,

85
00:05:03,013 --> 00:05:04,945
especially when tools have similar names.

86
00:05:06,150 --> 00:05:07,246
Think about our setup.

87
00:05:08,118 --> 00:05:11,240
Outlook, SharePoint,
Jira, Confluence, Slack.

88
00:05:11,253 --> 00:05:15,155
Almost every service has a
search, get, or list operation.

89
00:05:15,883 --> 00:05:18,414
With so many similar tools,
the model gets confused.

90
00:05:19,418 --> 00:05:21,381
So it's not just about wasting the tokens.

91
00:05:21,441 --> 00:05:25,253
Too many tools can also make
the agent less accurate, and

92
00:05:25,253 --> 00:05:28,055
that is a cost that doesn't show
up directly in your token bill

93
00:05:29,055 --> 00:05:31,149
So far, we have seen two problems.

94
00:05:31,570 --> 00:05:35,822
Tool definitions consume too
much context, and tool results

95
00:05:36,022 --> 00:05:37,647
keep passing through the model.

96
00:05:38,263 --> 00:05:39,954
So how do we fix both?

97
00:05:40,640 --> 00:05:42,591
The idea is surprisingly simple.

98
00:05:43,169 --> 00:05:46,416
Models are better at writing
code than calling tools.

99
00:05:48,073 --> 00:05:51,553
So why does writing code work better here?

100
00:05:52,450 --> 00:05:56,763
Normally, when a model calls a
tool, it generates a structured tool

101
00:05:56,801 --> 00:05:59,257
call using special tokens and JSON.

102
00:06:00,234 --> 00:06:05,104
But models have seen enormous
amounts of real code during training.

103
00:06:05,728 --> 00:06:10,457
So instead of making the model
generate tool calls, what if we

104
00:06:10,459 --> 00:06:15,162
give it a typed API and let it
write code to interact with tools?

105
00:06:16,192 --> 00:06:20,274
That code runs in a secure
sandbox, and only the necessary

106
00:06:20,274 --> 00:06:21,907
results come back to the model.

107
00:06:23,381 --> 00:06:27,092
Cloudflare called this Code Mode
in September twenty twenty-five.

108
00:06:27,582 --> 00:06:31,128
Anthropic explored a similar
approach in November and

109
00:06:31,128 --> 00:06:33,060
credited Cloudflare for the name.

110
00:06:33,844 --> 00:06:35,936
And this isn't just theoretical.

111
00:06:36,319 --> 00:06:39,935
We'll look at an agent using
this approach later in the talk.

112
00:06:41,618 --> 00:06:44,489
Before we go further,
let's try a quick question.

113
00:06:45,116 --> 00:06:48,054
Remember our earlier example
of moving a transcript from

114
00:06:48,054 --> 00:06:49,345
Google Drive to Salesforce?

115
00:06:49,986 --> 00:06:51,397
We have two options here.

116
00:06:52,306 --> 00:06:55,723
First option, make two tool calls
with the data passing through

117
00:06:55,723 --> 00:07:00,792
the model, and second, run one
script and return only a summary.

118
00:07:01,781 --> 00:07:04,010
Which approach uses fewer tokens?

119
00:07:04,306 --> 00:07:07,217
Type A or B in the chat,
and feel free to share why.

120
00:07:08,330 --> 00:07:09,632
Let's see how it works.

121
00:07:10,119 --> 00:07:11,402
The answer is B.

122
00:07:12,159 --> 00:07:15,037
And it's not just about saving tokens.

123
00:07:15,479 --> 00:07:19,426
We also reduced round trips
and avoid copying large amounts

124
00:07:19,429 --> 00:07:20,623
of data through the model.

125
00:07:22,337 --> 00:07:27,005
To understand why, let's look at
how Code Mode handles the same task.

126
00:07:27,674 --> 00:07:30,534
First, we import the tools as a library.

127
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:34,080
Then we fetch the transcript
from Google Drive and store it

128
00:07:34,080 --> 00:07:36,257
in variable inside the sandbox.

129
00:07:37,455 --> 00:07:41,828
The important part, the transcript
never enters the model's context.

130
00:07:43,104 --> 00:07:46,296
Next, we pass that variable
directly to Salesforce.

131
00:07:47,866 --> 00:07:54,478
Anthropic measured this exact workflow one
fifty K tokens down to just two thousand.

132
00:07:55,159 --> 00:07:57,594
That's a ninety-eight point
seven percent reduction.

133
00:07:58,311 --> 00:08:02,017
Same task, same result, but
without sending the entire

134
00:08:02,018 --> 00:08:03,340
transcript through the model.

135
00:08:03,885 --> 00:08:06,161
So that solves our second failure mode.

136
00:08:07,161 --> 00:08:09,923
But there's still one question.

137
00:08:10,477 --> 00:08:14,444
If we are not loading every tool
definition upfront, how does the

138
00:08:14,444 --> 00:08:16,488
model know which tools are available?

139
00:08:17,743 --> 00:08:18,857
The answer is simple.

140
00:08:20,124 --> 00:08:23,865
It explores them like a
developer exploring a code base.

141
00:08:24,831 --> 00:08:30,052
In Anthropic's approach, each server has
a folder, and each tool has its own file.

142
00:08:30,944 --> 00:08:35,495
The model can explore the available
folders, find Google Drive or Salesforce,

143
00:08:35,533 --> 00:08:38,275
and open only the tool files it needs.

144
00:08:39,901 --> 00:08:44,211
Each file contains a small typed
function with a description, so the

145
00:08:44,219 --> 00:08:49,307
model can understand how to use that
tool without loading everything upfront.

146
00:08:50,396 --> 00:08:53,722
This is what Anthropic calls
progressive disclosure.

147
00:08:54,323 --> 00:08:57,997
Instead of loading every tool
definition upfront, we only load

148
00:08:58,066 --> 00:08:59,780
what's needed when it's needed.

149
00:09:00,547 --> 00:09:04,799
So instead of paying the context
cost for every tool upfront, we only

150
00:09:04,799 --> 00:09:07,240
pay for the tools we actually use

151
00:09:08,240 --> 00:09:12,082
So we have seen how code mode
works, but what happens when we

152
00:09:12,111 --> 00:09:13,745
actually use it in production?

153
00:09:14,539 --> 00:09:19,127
We tested it on our agent connected to
Outlook, SharePoint, Jira, Confluence,

154
00:09:19,131 --> 00:09:20,768
Slack, and other developer tools.

155
00:09:21,519 --> 00:09:24,670
We measured it twice, first with
direct tool calls and then with the

156
00:09:24,670 --> 00:09:28,904
code mode using type tool definition
generated at connection time.

157
00:09:29,757 --> 00:09:31,524
Same tasks, same model.

158
00:09:32,082 --> 00:09:33,549
We focused on two metrics.

159
00:09:34,534 --> 00:09:37,500
First, prompt token before
the user's question.

160
00:09:38,300 --> 00:09:42,179
We log this on every request just
before adding the user message.

161
00:09:42,632 --> 00:09:45,867
It's a simple one-line change,
but it tells you how much

162
00:09:45,883 --> 00:09:47,343
context is already consumed.

163
00:09:48,352 --> 00:09:50,733
Second, tool selection accuracy.

164
00:09:51,261 --> 00:09:55,024
We measured how often the agent
picked the right tool against a set

165
00:09:55,026 --> 00:09:57,168
of tasks with expected tool choices.

166
00:09:57,934 --> 00:10:01,939
Together, these gave us a clear
picture of both context usage

167
00:10:02,069 --> 00:10:03,668
and tool selection accuracy.

168
00:10:05,093 --> 00:10:06,597
Now let's look at what changed.

169
00:10:07,112 --> 00:10:11,137
Here's our agent before the code
mode using one fifty K tokens

170
00:10:11,170 --> 00:10:12,578
just for tool definitions.

171
00:10:14,264 --> 00:10:18,768
And here's the same agent after the
code mode, just twenty-two K tokens

172
00:10:19,384 --> 00:10:23,100
covering the code tool, a short
index of connected tools, and the

173
00:10:23,102 --> 00:10:24,920
definitions needed for the tasks.

174
00:10:25,920 --> 00:10:29,133
That's an eighty-five percent reduction
with the same tools available.

175
00:10:30,299 --> 00:10:33,294
But the more interesting result
was tool selection accuracy.

176
00:10:33,784 --> 00:10:36,372
It went from forty-two
percent to eighty percent.

177
00:10:37,193 --> 00:10:42,505
Our explanation is that the model now sees
fewer similar tools at once and can focus

178
00:10:42,537 --> 00:10:45,058
on specific type definitions it needs.

179
00:10:45,521 --> 00:10:48,029
These results come from our
production agent with real

180
00:10:48,071 --> 00:10:49,891
tools and real-world complexity

181
00:10:50,891 --> 00:10:52,681
So we have seen the results.

182
00:10:53,031 --> 00:10:54,922
Now let's see code mode in action.

183
00:10:55,459 --> 00:10:59,916
For this demo, I'll be using Pi, an
open source terminal coding agent that

184
00:10:59,916 --> 00:11:01,970
supports code mode out of the box.

185
00:11:02,444 --> 00:11:04,255
Our production agent is different.

186
00:11:04,492 --> 00:11:07,825
But Pi makes it easy to
demonstrate the approach.

187
00:11:08,810 --> 00:11:12,689
We'll use, dummy Jira,
Confluence, and Slack MCP servers.

188
00:11:13,353 --> 00:11:17,533
And the important thing is this
approach isn't specific to Pi.

189
00:11:17,931 --> 00:11:21,964
It can work with any agent
that supports a stan- sandbox.

190
00:11:23,817 --> 00:11:25,276
This is Pi in a fresh session.

191
00:11:25,308 --> 00:11:29,101
I have connected Jira, Confluence, and
Slack with eighty-six tools in token.

192
00:11:29,697 --> 00:11:32,014
Before we begin, let's check the context.

193
00:11:32,425 --> 00:11:36,020
We are already at fifty-six K tokens
without even entering a prompt.

194
00:11:36,595 --> 00:11:38,988
Tool definition alone
account for fifty-four K.

195
00:11:39,602 --> 00:11:40,937
Now here's our task.

196
00:11:41,581 --> 00:11:47,101
Review five Jira bugs and two Confluence
runbooks, then post a summary to Slack.

197
00:11:47,782 --> 00:11:50,586
The agent starts by searching
Jira and Confluence.

198
00:11:51,086 --> 00:11:55,057
Notice how the context usage in
the bottom corner starts climbing.

199
00:11:55,842 --> 00:11:59,712
It then fetches all five bugs and
both runbooks, and posts the summary

200
00:11:59,717 --> 00:12:02,748
to Slack, and the task is complete.

201
00:12:04,500 --> 00:12:08,798
Five bugs reviewed, two runbooks
checked, and the summary posted

202
00:12:09,798 --> 00:12:12,785
But look at the context usage.

203
00:12:13,031 --> 00:12:15,058
We are already at 65%.

204
00:12:15,602 --> 00:12:17,942
Let's check where all those tokens went.

205
00:12:18,589 --> 00:12:21,622
Tools output alone consumed 42K tokens.

206
00:12:22,535 --> 00:12:29,346
Of those, 23K came from the Jira bugs,
nearly 10K from the runbooks, around 8K

207
00:12:29,347 --> 00:12:32,165
from searches, and another 1K from Slack.

208
00:12:33,005 --> 00:12:37,502
So including tool definitions,
outputs, and other messages, we are

209
00:12:37,559 --> 00:12:42,299
at roughly 98K tokens for a single
task, and that's our baseline.

210
00:12:43,067 --> 00:12:49,671
Now let's see how much of that we can save
with Code Mode So that was ninety-eight

211
00:12:49,699 --> 00:12:51,560
K tokens with direct tool calling, right?

212
00:12:51,928 --> 00:12:55,225
What do you think happens
when we switch to code mode?

213
00:12:55,953 --> 00:12:59,724
How many tokens do you think
we'll use for the exact same task?

214
00:13:00,615 --> 00:13:01,918
Drop your guess in the chat

215
00:13:02,918 --> 00:13:07,319
About 34K tokens, down from 98K.

216
00:13:07,413 --> 00:13:09,653
That's a 65% reduction.

217
00:13:10,300 --> 00:13:14,309
This is significant improvement,
but not quite what we expected.

218
00:13:14,737 --> 00:13:15,401
Let's see why

219
00:13:16,401 --> 00:13:19,943
Now, we are running the same task,
but this time with code mode enabled.

220
00:13:20,489 --> 00:13:23,904
As you can see, we are starting with
just one point two k tokens in the

221
00:13:23,905 --> 00:13:28,419
context, and instead of all eighty-six
tools, we now have just one code mode.

222
00:13:29,201 --> 00:13:32,567
Let's give it the same task:
summarize the high-priority open

223
00:13:32,567 --> 00:13:36,902
bugs in Jira, review the Confluence
runbooks, and post a summary to Slack.

224
00:13:37,611 --> 00:13:39,813
Now watch what happens exactly.

225
00:13:40,437 --> 00:13:44,517
Instead of making direct tool calls,
the agent starts writing and running

226
00:13:44,517 --> 00:13:46,129
scripts to interact with the tools.

227
00:13:46,733 --> 00:13:51,804
It searches Jira, retrieves the relevant
issues, gathers the information it needs.

228
00:13:52,407 --> 00:13:58,091
Along the way, it encounters an error, but
notice how it rec- recovers on its own and

229
00:13:58,093 --> 00:14:00,694
continues without any manual intervention.

230
00:14:01,574 --> 00:14:06,286
Once it has everything, it prepares the
summary and posts it to the Slack channel.

231
00:14:08,098 --> 00:14:10,047
And there we go.

232
00:14:10,316 --> 00:14:11,897
Same task, same result.

233
00:14:12,322 --> 00:14:13,697
But here's something interesting.

234
00:14:13,738 --> 00:14:17,127
If you look at the context usage
at the bottom, you'll notice it

235
00:14:17,133 --> 00:14:21,573
hasn't increased nearly as quickly
as it did with direct tool calling.

236
00:14:22,361 --> 00:14:24,258
Now let's run the context one more time.

237
00:14:24,771 --> 00:14:29,430
We are at around fifty k tokens, just
twenty-five percent of our context window.

238
00:14:29,975 --> 00:14:33,458
That's a significant reduction,
right, compared to our previous

239
00:14:33,458 --> 00:14:37,121
run, and we can see the breakdown
of where those tokens went.

240
00:14:37,626 --> 00:14:41,988
So code mode clearly makes a difference,
but there's still room to optimize

241
00:14:42,179 --> 00:14:43,980
what gets returned to the model.

242
00:14:44,980 --> 00:14:48,633
So where did those 31K tokens come from?

243
00:14:49,314 --> 00:14:51,815
Let's look at one of the
scripts from our demo.

244
00:14:52,236 --> 00:14:53,387
This is script seven.

245
00:14:54,339 --> 00:14:59,104
It makes five MCP calls in parallel,
fetching three Jira issues and two

246
00:14:59,108 --> 00:15:06,343
Confluence pages Then it picks only
the fields we need and trims the

247
00:15:06,344 --> 00:15:08,362
description to four hundred characters.

248
00:15:10,027 --> 00:15:14,826
And only that compact result
goes back to the model.

249
00:15:15,804 --> 00:15:18,876
This is exactly how we
want code mode to work.

250
00:15:19,394 --> 00:15:21,560
But not every script did this, right?

251
00:15:21,991 --> 00:15:25,942
One returned full Jira issues
and Confluence search results

252
00:15:26,265 --> 00:15:27,999
around twenty-six k tokens.

253
00:15:28,526 --> 00:15:30,732
Another returned complete
tool description.

254
00:15:31,525 --> 00:15:34,988
That's where most of those
thirty-one k tokens came from.

255
00:15:35,571 --> 00:15:40,860
So code mode saves us from loading
tool definitions upfront, but what

256
00:15:40,866 --> 00:15:43,590
comes back to the model still matters.

257
00:15:45,036 --> 00:15:47,923
So how does Pi make all of this work?

258
00:15:48,914 --> 00:15:54,132
The model writes JavaScript, which runs
inside a QuickJS sandbox with no

259
00:15:54,139 --> 00:15:56,169
direct file system or network access.

260
00:15:56,672 --> 00:16:01,678
It calls MCP tools through the tools API
and can run multiple calls in parallel.

261
00:16:02,473 --> 00:16:06,153
Only the script's return
output goes back to the model.

262
00:16:07,533 --> 00:16:09,084
And here's the key part.

263
00:16:09,850 --> 00:16:12,356
Tool definitions aren't loaded upfront.

264
00:16:12,832 --> 00:16:17,388
Instead, the model discovers
what it needs using search tools.

265
00:16:17,921 --> 00:16:22,515
It's the same progressive disclosure
approach we discussed earlier, just

266
00:16:22,516 --> 00:16:24,602
using search instead of folders.

267
00:16:25,138 --> 00:16:30,172
So code mode clearly saves context,
but as we saw in the demo, it

268
00:16:30,175 --> 00:16:33,807
doesn't solve everything, and
that's where things get interesting.

269
00:16:34,467 --> 00:16:36,758
Nishant, over to you for the honest part.

270
00:16:38,650 --> 00:16:39,253
Thanks, Sonam.

271
00:16:39,551 --> 00:16:41,485
So we have seen what code mode can do.

272
00:16:41,948 --> 00:16:45,119
Now let's talk about where
things didn't go as planned.

273
00:16:46,518 --> 00:16:48,737
We ran into five main challenges.

274
00:16:49,485 --> 00:16:52,962
First, the model sometimes
called methods that didn't exist.

275
00:16:53,469 --> 00:16:58,289
For example, it would guess get
issue by key for Jira even when

276
00:16:58,477 --> 00:17:00,667
that's wasn't the actual method name.

277
00:17:01,618 --> 00:17:05,454
The sandbox returns an error with the
closest matching method, so we treat

278
00:17:05,455 --> 00:17:07,516
this as a retry rather than a failure.

279
00:17:08,872 --> 00:17:10,829
Second, stale tool definitions.

280
00:17:11,404 --> 00:17:15,338
When an MCP server changed,
our generated code could break.

281
00:17:15,948 --> 00:17:18,740
So we regenerate the tool
definitions at connection time

282
00:17:18,821 --> 00:17:20,375
instead of storing them permanently.

283
00:17:21,692 --> 00:17:24,055
Third, the token savings disappeared.

284
00:17:24,480 --> 00:17:29,446
Initially, we had one large types file
per server, which was basically the

285
00:17:29,449 --> 00:17:31,532
same schema dump in a different format.

286
00:17:31,919 --> 00:17:33,114
The fix was simple.

287
00:17:33,372 --> 00:17:34,794
One file per tool.

288
00:17:36,199 --> 00:17:39,333
Fourth, too much output
coming back to the model.

289
00:17:39,490 --> 00:17:40,880
You saw this in the demo as well.

290
00:17:41,180 --> 00:17:44,903
Our first run consumed around
forty-five k tokens because the

291
00:17:44,903 --> 00:17:46,795
model returned full Jira results.

292
00:17:47,489 --> 00:17:51,360
We added an instruction to return
only the necessary fields, which

293
00:17:51,379 --> 00:17:53,002
brought it down to thirty-four k.

294
00:17:53,607 --> 00:17:55,027
Better, but still not ideal.

295
00:17:55,599 --> 00:17:59,835
Code mode removes the tool definitions,
but we still need to control the output.

296
00:18:00,908 --> 00:18:03,164
And fifth, debugging became harder.

297
00:18:03,916 --> 00:18:07,446
When multiple tool calls happen
inside a script, it's harder

298
00:18:07,446 --> 00:18:08,720
to trace what went wrong.

299
00:18:09,371 --> 00:18:13,455
So we log each tool call inside
the sandbox, including its

300
00:18:13,526 --> 00:18:15,408
arguments and result size.

301
00:18:17,104 --> 00:18:19,886
Now, code mode isn't
always the right choice.

302
00:18:20,562 --> 00:18:24,225
If your agent only uses three
tools for simple tasks, adding

303
00:18:24,228 --> 00:18:26,179
a sandbox might not be worth it.

304
00:18:27,099 --> 00:18:30,287
If you don't have a trusted
sandbox, that's another concern.

305
00:18:30,923 --> 00:18:34,070
Running model-generated code
requires proper isolation,

306
00:18:34,215 --> 00:18:36,081
resource limits, and monitoring.

307
00:18:37,338 --> 00:18:41,383
And if your workflow needs human
approval at every step, batching

308
00:18:41,386 --> 00:18:44,105
multiple operations into one
script can make that difficult.

309
00:18:45,066 --> 00:18:48,888
In those cases, tool search
might be a simpler alternative.

310
00:18:49,307 --> 00:18:53,608
You keep normal tool calling, but load
only the relevant tool definitions

311
00:18:53,641 --> 00:18:55,147
instead of everything upfront.

312
00:18:56,206 --> 00:19:00,961
So here's a question for you: What would
stop you from using code mode at work?

313
00:19:01,620 --> 00:19:02,643
Let us know in the chat.

314
00:19:04,636 --> 00:19:08,425
So if you want to try this yourself,
here are three simple steps.

315
00:19:09,901 --> 00:19:11,331
First, measure.

316
00:19:12,022 --> 00:19:15,998
Log how many prompt tokens are
consumed before the user's question.

317
00:19:16,600 --> 00:19:19,948
If it's already in the tens of
thousands, that's worth investing.

318
00:19:21,150 --> 00:19:22,746
Second, try code mode.

319
00:19:23,160 --> 00:19:27,193
You can use Pi hyphen hyphen
tools is equal to code mode, the

320
00:19:27,194 --> 00:19:31,111
Cloudflare's approach, or even build
your own type tool definitions.

321
00:19:32,231 --> 00:19:33,672
Third, measure again.

322
00:19:33,941 --> 00:19:37,119
Same task, same model,
compare the results.

323
00:19:37,906 --> 00:19:42,106
And remember the three numbers we started
with, one point one seven million to

324
00:19:42,108 --> 00:19:47,705
one K, one fifty K to two K, and our
own result, one fifty K to twenty-two K.

325
00:19:48,683 --> 00:19:51,447
Three different measurements,
but the same underlying idea.

326
00:19:52,984 --> 00:19:56,214
And with that, we have come
to the end of our talk.

327
00:19:56,710 --> 00:19:58,120
Thank you so much for watching.

328
00:19:58,509 --> 00:20:01,705
Our LinkedIn QR codes are on
screen, and you'll find all

329
00:20:01,714 --> 00:20:03,058
the sources in the description.

330
00:20:03,729 --> 00:20:06,198
Before we wrap up, one
last question for you guys.

331
00:20:06,758 --> 00:20:11,116
Tools or code, which approach
will your next agent use?

332
00:20:11,788 --> 00:20:13,296
Drop your thoughts in the chat.

333
00:20:13,574 --> 00:20:18,211
We are here for you for the next hour,
and we would love to hear from you back.

334
00:20:18,989 --> 00:20:19,397
Thank you.

335
00:20:19,975 --> 00:20:20,789
Thank you so much.

